title: Barracuda 44-Hour Intrusion
page: breaches/barracuda-44-hour-intrusion.html
as-of: 2026-11-23
promise: A cloud key Barracuda gave an AI vendor and did not retire was exposed in the vendor's compromise; with it, an attacker chained secret to secret through parts of Barracuda's cloud, per Barracuda.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: ~44 | hours, in Barracuda's words | First to last recorded activity: about 43 hours (the page's arithmetic)
stat: ~10 | cloud accounts touched, per Barracuda | Cloud accounts only; other systems on the chain are not counted
stat: 5 | platforms crossed "in less than two days" | Not listed by Barracuda
No actor named. Customers' XDR credentials: no direct evidence they were read, and logging too thin to rule it out.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with, and with whose figures they are.

Barracuda says the attacker "touched about 10 cloud accounts in roughly 44 hours." Its first and last recorded activity, at 04:33 UTC on November 1 and 23:31 UTC on November 2, are about 43 hours apart. That measured span is the page's own arithmetic; 44 hours is Barracuda's framing.

About 10 counts cloud accounts only. The data analytics system, the source-code token, the build workflow and the Kubernetes clusters are further systems on the chain. Barracuda also says the attacker crossed five platforms in less than two days, and does not list them.

Barracuda names no actor. It matches the tooling to ARTEX, an open-source AI-driven penetration-testing framework, and says the match tells it how the attacker operated, not who it was.

One system the attacker reached stores credentials Barracuda XDR uses to collect data from customers' connected systems. Barracuda found no direct evidence they were viewed or accessed, and says logging there was not detailed enough to rule it out. Say both halves.

The page draws almost entirely on Barracuda's own account, and no independent forensic report has been published.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: October | The vendor's development environment is compromised, exposing the key
event: November 1, 04:33 UTC | First call with the key; mapping its reach within minutes
event: November 2, early hours | Short-lived branches print the build system's secrets
event: November 2, 23:31 UTC | Last recorded attacker activity
event: November 4 | An engineer reports; the vendor calls; container removed that evening, Pacific time
event: November 10 to 13 | Advisory, the CISO's post, and the update naming the key's source
All dates are 2026. The activity had stopped before it was reported, per Barracuda. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every date on this slide is one the page gives, and the markers are evenly spaced, not to scale.

Barracuda's post of November 11 places the vendor's compromise in October. Its update of November 13 says the key sat in a configuration file in the vendor's GitHub repository, exposed when the vendor's development environment was compromised.

At 04:33 UTC on November 1 the attacker made its first call with the key, and within minutes was mapping what it could reach. Over the next 24 hours it used each secret it found to reach the next system. In the early hours of November 2, in just under four hours, it pushed short-lived branches so a build workflow would print the build system's secrets. The last recorded activity was at 23:31 UTC on November 2.

On November 4 an engineer reported unusual activity on their own account, and the vendor contacted Barracuda about its own incident. Barracuda removed the container that evening, Pacific time, and rotated credentials across every system involved.

The Trust Center advisory came on November 10, the CISO's post on November 11, and the update confirming the key's source on November 13.

== Barracuda's claims, and the page's caveats
layout: table
row: Barracuda's claim | The caveat the page keeps
row: The tooling matches ARTEX | The tool, not the attacker; no actor named
row: About 10 cloud accounts, roughly 44 hours | Cloud accounts only; about 43 hours, first to last
row: Financial: crypto assets, reusable credentials | A third, AI service credentials, "cannot be ruled out"
row: No direct evidence XDR credentials were read | Logging too thin to rule it out
row: Key exposed in the vendor's compromise | By whom, and whether the repository was public: not stated
Nearly all from Barracuda; no independent forensic report published.
notes:
This table keeps each claim beside the caveat the page keeps with it. The page draws almost entirely on Barracuda's own account. No independent forensic report has been published, and the press coverage and the Cloud Security Alliance's research note rest on Barracuda's posts.

ARTEX is Barracuda's identification of the tool from what the attacker left behind: probe names, other indicators, the order of its work and built-in routines for hunting credentials and wallets. Barracuda reports no recovered binary, console or model, and says the match tells it how the attacker operated, not who it was.

About 10 counts cloud accounts only. The 44 hours is Barracuda's framing; the span from first to last recorded activity is about 43 hours, by the page's arithmetic.

The five platforms, on the At a glance slide, are not listed by Barracuda. The CSA note lists five systems on the chain, but does not say they are Barracuda's five.

Barracuda reads the activity as financially motivated, and adds that a third motive cannot be ruled out.

On the XDR credentials, there is no evidence they were read, and Barracuda cannot show that they were not.

No source says who compromised the vendor, whether it was the same attacker, or whether the repository was public.

== The kill chain, stages 01 to 06
layout: flow
step: 01 | Credential Exposure | A vendor-held key never retired | T1199
step: 02 | Resource Development | Tooling matched to ARTEX | T1588.007
step: 03 | Initial Access | First call; secret stores read | T1078.004
step: 04 | Lateral Movement | Each secret opens the next system | T1550.001
step: 05 | Execution | Branches print the build's secrets | T1677
step: 06 | Credential Access | State files, metadata, service accounts | T1552.005
Stages 06 to 09 carry no times; the order is the post's and may not be the real one.
notes:
The page has eleven stages, so the chain takes two slides. Each step shows its phase and one ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stage 01 is the exposure: a cloud access key Barracuda gave an AI vendor for a project and did not retire, exposed when the vendor's development environment was compromised in October. Stage 02 is the tooling, which Barracuda matches to ARTEX from fingerprints. Stage 03 is the first call with the key, at 04:33 UTC on November 1, and the reading of stored secrets. Stage 04 is the chain itself: each secret opening the next system, from cloud accounts to a data analytics system, a source-code token and Kubernetes clusters. Stage 05 is a build workflow on short-lived branches that printed the build system's secrets. Stage 06 is credential access from state files, metadata services and Kubernetes service accounts.

The post gives no times for stages 06 to 09. The page follows the order in which the post describes them, which may not be the order in which they happened.

== The kill chain, stages 07 to 11
layout: flow
step: 07 | Discovery | Servers searched for wallets | T1083
step: 08 | Persistence | A host-mounting container | T1610
step: 09 | Persistence | Admin identity attempted, blocked | T1136.003
step: 10 | Exposure | Customers' XDR credentials in reach | -
step: 11 | Detection | Reported after it stopped | -
Stages 07 to 09 carry no times; the order is the post's and may not be the real one. No technique is mapped to 10 or 11.
notes:
The second half runs from the hunt for wallets to the report.

Stage 07 is discovery: on a group of servers where it gained shell access, the attacker searched for wallet material, and there was none. Stage 08 is what Barracuda calls the backdoor, a read-only container that mounted a server's file system and harvested its credentials. Stage 09 is an attempt to create an administrative cloud identity, which was blocked.

Stages 07 to 09 carry no times in Barracuda's post, so the arrows show the post's order, not a confirmed sequence. Stage 10 is the system storing credentials Barracuda XDR uses for customers' connected systems. The page maps no technique to it, because no collection is shown. Stage 11 is detection: an engineer's report and the vendor's call on November 4, after the activity had stopped.

The next slides take each stage in turn.

== A key handed to a vendor, never retired
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Credential Exposure
technique: T1552.001 | Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files
technique: T1199 | Trusted Relationship
fact: Key | a "cloud access key"; type, cloud and scope not disclosed
fact: Not retired | when the vendor finished the work (date not disclosed)
fact: Where it sat | a configuration file in the vendor's GitHub repository
fact: Exposed | in the vendor's October compromise; by whom not stated
"That is on us," the CISO wrote. The key outlived the engagement it was issued for.
notes:
Barracuda's account begins with its own decision. As part of a previous project it gave a cloud access key to a third-party vendor, which it describes only as "a company working in AI", and did not retire the key when the work ended. "That is on us," the CISO wrote. Barracuda does not name the vendor or the project, and does not say what kind of key it was, which cloud issued it or what it could reach.

In October the vendor's development environment was compromised. Barracuda's November 13 update says the key had been stored in a configuration file in the vendor's GitHub repository, exposed in that compromise. Other Barracuda credentials in the same file had already expired or have since been revoked.

No source says who compromised the vendor, whether it was the same attacker, or whether the repository was public. The page notes that Barracuda's wording points away from a key found by searching public GitHub, but it does not say the repository was private.

ATT&CK has no technique for a key that outlived its project. The page maps the exposure to Credentials In Files and the use of a third party's credential to Trusted Relationship, and calls the failure underneath one of lifecycle.

== Tooling Barracuda identifies as ARTEX, from fingerprints
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1588.007 | Obtain Capabilities: Artificial Intelligence
fact: Matched on | probe names, indicators, order of work, hunting routines
fact: Resource names | "i" plus digits, no hyphen: i31911, i28324probe, i19engx1
fact: Recovered | no binary, console or model reported
No actor named. Barracuda: "Because many groups use ARTEX, the match tells us how the attacker operated, not who it was."
notes:
Barracuda identifies the tooling from what it left behind. Its post says the probe names, the other indicators it collected, the order of the attacker's work and its built-in routines for hunting credentials and wallets all match ARTEX, an open-source AI-driven penetration-testing framework. Its advisory lists naming fingerprints, which it calls consistent with publicly reported characteristics of ARTEX. It reports no recovered binary, console or model, so this is an identification from fingerprints.

The FBI's FLASH of November 13 describes ARTEX as an open-source, LLM-driven agentic platform, released on GitHub in July 2026 and made unavailable on October 8 after reports of abuse, which still circulates through forks, archived copies and a commercial version. LLM stands for large language model. The FLASH does not mention Barracuda.

Because many groups use ARTEX, Barracuda says, the match tells it how the attacker operated, not who it was. It names no actor, and neither does this deck. On Barracuda's identification, the page maps the tooling to Obtain Capabilities: Artificial Intelligence.

The hunting slide near the end lists the published file names, addresses and user agents, with Barracuda's caveat.

== The first call, then secret after secret
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
technique: T1580 | Cloud Infrastructure Discovery
technique: T1555.006 | Credentials from Password Stores: Cloud Secrets Management Stores
fact: First call | November 1, 04:33 UTC; mapping its reach "within minutes"
fact: Secrets read | among them AWS access keys, Azure service principal secrets
fact: Not disclosed | provider, key type, account, permissions, secret store
An outside party's key still had read access to stored secrets.
notes:
Barracuda says the attacker gained access "using a valid cloud access key", made its first call with it at 04:33 UTC on November 1, and within minutes was mapping what the key could reach. It then used the key to read stored secrets, which included AWS access keys and Azure service principal secrets. The post puts "within minutes" on the mapping and gives no time for the secret reads.

Barracuda does not say which cloud provider issued the key, what kind it was, which account it belonged to, what permissions it held, or which secret store was read.

One published indicator is the user agent Boto3/1.43.122, the AWS SDK for Python. The page reads that as suggesting some of the activity called AWS APIs, and labels it its own inference, because the indicators are not tied to steps. Keep that label.

The page maps the key's use to Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts, the mapping of its reach to Cloud Infrastructure Discovery, and the secret reads to Cloud Secrets Management Stores.

Its suggestion: alert on the first use of a dormant key and on use from a new network, and treat an outside identity that maps its own reach and then reads secret after secret as an incident.

== Each secret opens the next system
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
technique: T1550.001 | Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token
fact: Next 24 hours | cloud accounts, analytics system, source-code token, Kubernetes
fact: Scale | "about 10" cloud accounts only; "five platforms", not listed
fact: Also reached | the store of Barracuda XDR integration credentials (stage 10)
Barracuda: "No single credential should open the next door, and the one after that."
notes:
Barracuda describes the core of the intrusion as a chain. Over the 24 hours after the first call, the attacker used each secret it found to reach the next system: from Barracuda's cloud accounts to a data analytics system, from there to a source-code access token, and from there to Kubernetes clusters. Its advisory adds that between November 1 and November 2 the attacker had access to the system storing credentials Barracuda XDR uses, which stage 10 covers.

Be careful with the scale. About 10 counts cloud accounts only; the analytics system, the token, the build workflow and the clusters are further systems. Barracuda says the attacker crossed five platforms in less than two days and does not list them. The Cloud Security Alliance's note lists five systems on the chain, without saying they are Barracuda's five.

Barracuda does not say what the analytics system was, what the token allowed or which account held what, and it does not say whether any source code was copied.

At each stage the attacker found credentials for the next system in reach of the one it was in. The page maps the cloud credentials to Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts and the source-code token to Application Access Token.

== Short-lived branches that print the build's secrets
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Execution
technique: T1677 | Poisoned Pipeline Execution
technique: T1027 | Obfuscated Files or Information
fact: When | November 2, early hours, "just under four hours"
fact: Pushed to | "a handful of repositories"
fact: Workflow files | i19ghci.yml, i19ci2.yml, on branches i19ghci- and i19ci2-
fact: Output | the build system's secrets, encoded, printed into the job log
Encoded so masking would not hide them. Barracuda does not say which secrets.
notes:
Barracuda says that in the early hours of November 2, in just under four hours, the attacker pushed short-lived branches to a handful of repositories to trigger a build workflow written to print the build system's secrets into the job log. The secrets were encoded so that the log's usual masking would not hide them. Barracuda does not say which credential pushed the branches; the source-code token in stage 04 is the one its account names.

Barracuda does not name the CI product. The workflow files sit in GitHub, and YAML workflow files on branches are how GitHub Actions is configured, so the page reads the build system as GitHub Actions. Keep that labelled as the page's reading. There, the runner masks each secret's value and a set of standard encodings of it, and GitHub's documentation warns that redaction is not guaranteed. A secret encoded some other way passes through.

Barracuda makes no statement either way about whether anything from these branches reached a product build or release, and gives no count of repositories.

The page maps the workflow files to Poisoned Pipeline Execution and the encoding to Obfuscated Files or Information, which ATT&CK v19 places under Stealth.

== What poisoned pipeline execution is
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK, Poisoned Pipeline Execution (T1677) | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1677/
MITRE ATT&CK: adversaries may manipulate CI/CD processes by injecting malicious code into the build process.

Direct: the actor modifies the CI configuration file. Indirect: code goes into files it references. Public: a pull request from a fork triggers the pipeline.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general idea from MITRE ATT&CK's entry for T1677, which ATT&CK places under the Execution tactic. ATT&CK expands CI/CD as continuous integration / continuous development.

ATT&CK describes three ways to poison a pipeline. In direct pipeline execution, the actor modifies the CI configuration file itself, and may include a command to send credentials used in the build to a remote server or export them as a workflow artifact. In indirect pipeline execution, the actor injects code into files the configuration references, such as makefiles, scripts, unit tests or linters. In public pipeline execution, the actor has no direct access to the repository and instead opens a malicious pull request from a fork that triggers part of the pipeline.

The page maps stage 05 to this technique: workflow files the attacker pushed on short-lived branches, written to print the build system's secrets. The page does not place it in one of ATT&CK's three scenarios, so read this slide as the general idea only.

== State files, metadata services and service accounts
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.001 | Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files
technique: T1552.005 | Unsecured Credentials: Cloud Instance Metadata API
technique: T1528 | Steal Application Access Token
fact: Decrypted | IaC state files, "which often contain credentials"
fact: Credentials from | server metadata services; Kubernetes service accounts
fact: Not disclosed | how they were decrypted, which tool wrote them, which servers
Each store held credentials, and the attacker could reach what unlocked it.
notes:
In the part of its post on what the attacker was looking for, Barracuda says it hunted credentials and secrets everywhere. It decrypted infrastructure-as-code state files, which often contain credentials, and pulled credentials from server metadata services and from Kubernetes service accounts.

The post gives no times for this or for stages 07 to 09, and the page follows the order in which the post describes them, which may not be the order in which they happened.

The page explains the three stores in its own words. A Terraform state file, for example, records the values of the resources it manages, secrets included, so whatever decrypts it works as one more credential. A metadata service hands an instance's role credentials to any process that can reach it. A Kubernetes service account token is what a pod presents to the cluster's API.

Barracuda does not say how the state files were decrypted, which tool wrote them, or which servers and clusters were involved.

The page suggests alerting on reads of state files or their encryption keys by any identity other than the deployment pipeline, and on metadata calls from containers with no need for node credentials.

== Servers searched for wallets that were not there
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Discovery
technique: T1059 | Command and Scripting Interpreter
technique: T1083 | File and Directory Discovery
technique: T1552.007 | Unsecured Credentials: Container API
fact: Where | "a group of servers where it gained shell access"
fact: Variables | WALLET, MNEMONIC, SEED, PRIV
fact: Also sought | Ethereum keystores, extended private keys, seed phrase files, wallet-named Kubernetes secrets
fact: Motive (Barracuda) | financial (crypto, reusable credentials); a third, AI service credentials, "cannot be ruled out"
notes:
Barracuda says that on a group of servers where it gained shell access, the attacker's commands searched explicitly for environment variables named WALLET, MNEMONIC, SEED and PRIV, Ethereum keystore files, extended private keys, seed phrase files, and Kubernetes secrets with wallet-related names. "No wallet material exists on those systems," Barracuda says. It does not say how the attacker gained shell access or on which servers.

Barracuda reads it as consistent with a financially motivated attacker seeking cryptocurrency assets and reusable credentials, and adds: "A third motive cannot be ruled out." Some of the secrets in the attacker's reach were credentials for AI services.

Barracuda compares its intrusion with Railway's, one of the AI developer platforms that disclosed incidents in October. In Barracuda's reading, Railway's report describes a chain much like its own: an initial foothold, a copy of internal source code, a misconfiguration and a credential that unlocked customers' stored secrets. That copy belongs to Railway's chain; Barracuda does not say any of its own code was copied. Railway's November 10 report says its own investigation suggests AI provider keys were the attack's primary target. The comparison is of shape and motive; neither company says the same attacker was involved.

The page maps this to Command and Scripting Interpreter, File and Directory Discovery and Container API.

== What Barracuda calls the backdoor
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Persistence
technique: T1610 | Deploy Container
technique: T1611 | Escape to Host
fact: Deployed | a container on one of Barracuda's Kubernetes clusters
fact: Mount | the underlying server's file system, read-only
fact: Harvested | "that server's credentials"
fact: Removed | the evening of November 4, Pacific time
Read-only stops a container from changing the host's files, not from reading them (the page's explanation).
notes:
Barracuda's post calls this the backdoor. Using access to one of its Kubernetes clusters, the attacker deployed a container that mounted the underlying server's file system. The container was restricted to read-only access, "but that was sufficient to harvest that server's credentials." It stayed until Barracuda removed it on the evening of November 4, Pacific time.

Barracuda does not say which cluster, which identity deployed the container, how the mount was set up, or which credentials it harvested.

The page explains why read-only was enough: it stops a container from changing the host's files, not from reading them, and a server's file system holds the credentials the server itself uses. It adds that an admission policy refusing host path mounts and privileged pods would most likely have refused this pod, because the baseline Pod Security Standard forbids both. The next slide explains that standard.

Barracuda says it is restricting privileged workloads and limiting which services can read secrets, and has deployed runtime threat detection across its clusters. The page maps the deployment to Deploy Container and the host mount to Escape to Host.

== What the baseline Pod Security Standard forbids
layout: background
source: Kubernetes, Pod Security Standards | https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/
Kubernetes defines three policies: Privileged, Baseline and Restricted. Baseline is "minimally restrictive" and prevents known privilege escalations.

Among its controls: privileged pods "must be disallowed", and "HostPath volumes must be forbidden."
notes:
This is a background slide, from the Kubernetes documentation on Pod Security Standards, in general terms.

Kubernetes defines three policies. Privileged is unrestricted and allows known privilege escalations. Baseline is minimally restrictive: it is aimed at ease of adoption for common containerized workloads while preventing known privilege escalations, and it allows the default pod configuration. Restricted is heavily restricted, following current pod hardening best practices.

Two Baseline controls matter here. Privileged pods disable most security mechanisms and must be disallowed. HostPath volumes must be forbidden.

The page cites this documentation, and says an admission policy refusing host path mounts and privileged pods would most likely have refused the container in stage 08. Barracuda does not say how the mount was set up, so read this slide as the general standard, not as a finding about Barracuda's clusters.

== An administrative identity, attempted and blocked
layout: stage
stage: 09
phase: Persistence
technique: T1136.003 | Create Account: Cloud Account
fact: Attempted | a new administrative cloud identity for itself
fact: Result | blocked; the blocking control is not named
fact: Not stated | which cloud, or which credential made the attempt
The one point in Barracuda's account where it says a control stopped the attacker.
notes:
In the part of its post on the backdoor, Barracuda says the attacker also tried to create a new administrative cloud identity for itself, and that the attempt was blocked. It does not say which cloud, which credential made the attempt or what blocked it.

It is the one point in Barracuda's account where it says a control stopped the attacker.

The Cloud Security Alliance's research note recommends making that kind of block deliberate: a preventive deny on creating new administrative identities or attaching privileged policies, with a break-glass path that needs a second approver. The page adds a suggestion of its own: alert on every denied attempt, since a denial shows a stolen credential trying to escalate.

The page maps the attempt to Create Account: Cloud Account, a Persistence technique.

== The store holding customers' XDR credentials
layout: stage
stage: 10
phase: Exposure
fact: Held | credentials XDR uses for customers' Microsoft 365, Google Workspace and more
fact: Access | the attacker reached the system between November 1 and 2
fact: Evidence of reads | none found
fact: Ruled out | no; "logging in that environment was not detailed enough"
fact: Customers | notified API integration users; how many is not disclosed
No evidence the credentials were read, and Barracuda cannot show they were not.
notes:
Barracuda's advisory says one of the affected systems stores credentials Barracuda XDR uses to collect data from customers' connected systems, such as Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, endpoint security platforms and other integrated services, and that the attacker had access to it between November 1 and November 2.

Its investigation found no direct evidence that the attacker viewed or accessed those credentials. "However, logging in that environment was not detailed enough to rule it out." Say both halves together.

Barracuda treated them as possibly exposed. It asked the customers concerned, organizations using an API integration for Managed XDR Cloud or Monitored Endpoint, to revoke and recreate their integration credentials and review activity in their connected systems since November 1. It has not said how many it notified, and no source reports a customer being compromised.

Barracuda is moving the credentials to a store that stays protected even if cluster administration is compromised, which on the page's reading means a cluster administrator could read them before. It has enabled full audit logging on the affected environments.

The page maps no technique here, because no collection is shown.

== Reported only after it had stopped
layout: stage
stage: 11
phase: Detection
fact: Last activity | November 2, 23:31 UTC
fact: November 4 | an engineer reports unusual activity on their own account
fact: Same day | the vendor contacts Barracuda about its own incident
fact: That evening | container removed (Pacific time); credential rotation follows
Barracuda: "By the time the activity was reported, it had already stopped."
notes:
The intrusion was reported only after it had stopped. Barracuda's post says detection started with an engineer and information from a partner. On November 4 an engineer noticed unusual activity on their own account and reported it, and the same day the vendor contacted Barracuda about its own incident. The vendor did not believe Barracuda had been affected, but shared what it knew anyway.

The post says the partner's information arrived two days after the attack began. By Barracuda's own dates, the vendor's call came about three days after the first call and more than a day after the last recorded activity. That is the page's arithmetic; keep it labelled.

Barracuda removed the container that evening, Pacific time, and rotated credentials across every system involved. Its November 13 update confirmed the initial access path and said it had retired two internal automation servers whose credentials were exposed. Its investigation is ongoing.

Barracuda's lesson from this timeline is that detect-and-respond, as the primary strategy, is dead, and that the focus must move to prevention and recovery: removing standing credentials, shrinking each credential's blast radius, and recovering quickly.

== The lesson sits in step 01, and Barracuda states it
layout: text
In the CISO's words:
- "Short-lived, narrowly scoped credentials would have stopped this intrusion at its first step."
- "No single credential should open the next door, and the one after that."
notes:
The page puts the lesson in step 01, and Barracuda states it itself. Of the key it did not retire, the CISO wrote: "That is on us." And: "Short-lived, narrowly scoped credentials would have stopped this intrusion at its first step."

On the page's reading, a key handed to a vendor stays usable by anyone who reaches the vendor's copy until it is expired, so the vendor's security is part of yours for as long as the key lives. Keep that labelled as the page's reading. Barracuda does not say what the key could reach or why it was not retired.

What followed shows the second failure Barracuda names: "No single credential should open the next door, and the one after that." The first key reached secrets that opened the next system, and those opened the one after.

Barracuda's remediation now includes removing long-lived and over-privileged access keys, and expiring credentials held by vendors when their engagements end.

== Defenses against stages 01 to 06
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Expire vendor credentials when the work ends | 01
row: Short-lived, scoped vendor access | 01 | 03
row: No single credential opens the next door | 03 | 04
row: Keep build secrets from pushable branches | 05
row: Refuse host mounts; keep node credentials from pods | 06
A defense is marked only where its wording counters that stage. None counters stage 02; two mention its indicators.
notes:
The page gives eight defenses. Five reach stages 01 to 06, and a cell is marked only where the defense's wording counters that stage, not where it merely mentions something from it.

Expiring vendor credentials counters the key not retired at stage 01. Short-lived vendor access counters the standing key, stage 01, and says a static key should have no read access to secret stores, stage 03. No single credential opening the next door counters the secret reads at stage 03 and the chain to the next system at stage 04. Keeping build secrets from pushable branches counters the workflow on a short-lived branch, stage 05; its advice to scope source-code tokens so they cannot push is counted there too, where the branches were pushed, not at stage 04. Refusing host mounts counters metadata credential theft with IMDSv2 and hop limits, stage 06, though no defense addresses its state files or service accounts.

Now look at the empty column. No defense counters stage 02, the tooling. Two mention its indicators, the build-secrets defense and the defense on telling others, but only as markers to hunt with or share, not as a counter to the tooling.

== Defenses against stages 07 to 11
layout: matrix
columns: 07 | 08 | 09 | 10 | 11
row: Refuse host mounts; keep node credentials from pods | 08
row: A preventive deny on new admin identities | 09
row: Log reads where credentials are stored | 10
row: Tell others what was in reach | 10 | 11
None of the page's eight defenses counters stage 07, the search for wallets.
notes:
Four defenses reach the second half of the chain.

Refusing host mounts at admission counters the read-only container that mounted a server's file system, stage 08. The preventive deny counters the attempt to create an administrative cloud identity, stage 09. Logging reads where credentials are stored counters stage 10's logging gap, where Barracuda could not rule out that customers' XDR credentials were read. Telling others what was in reach covers stage 10 too, since it says to tell another company which of its credentials were in reach, and stage 11, where the vendor's call reached Barracuda the same day an engineer reported unusual activity.

Now look at the empty column. None of the page's eight defenses counters stage 07, the shell commands that searched servers for wallet material. With stage 02 on the previous slide, that makes two gaps, and stage 07 is the second discussion question.

The next four slides take the defenses in pairs.

== Expire vendor keys; prefer short-lived access
layout: compare
col: Expire at the work's end | Set expiry at issue, tied to the engagement's end | Check last use: AWS's IAM credential report; Entra ID service principal sign-in logs | CSA: inventory keys held by vendors; revoke, not merely disable
col: Short-lived, not standing | Barracuda: short-lived, scoped credentials "would have stopped this intrusion at its first step" | Federation leaves nothing long-lived to find (the page's reading) | A static key: no secret-store reads, vendor's network where the cloud allows
Setting the expiry and checking last use are the page's suggestions.
notes:
Two defenses for stage 01.

Expire a vendor's credentials when the vendor's work ends. Barracuda's remediation now includes exactly that. The page suggests setting the expiry when the credential is issued, tied to the engagement's end date, and checking before then whether it is still in use: in AWS, aws iam get-access-key-last-used and the IAM credential report give last-used dates for access keys, and in Entra ID the service principal sign-in logs do the same for application credentials. The Cloud Security Alliance's first two recommended actions are to inventory every long-lived cloud key, API token and service principal secret held outside your control, starting with those issued to vendors, contractors and AI service providers, and to revoke, not merely disable, credentials tied to completed engagements.

Give vendors short-lived access instead of a standing key. Barracuda says short-lived, narrowly scoped credentials would have stopped this intrusion at its first step. On the page's reading, a credential issued through federation each time the vendor needs access leaves nothing long-lived to find in a configuration file. Where a static key cannot be avoided, scope it to what the project needs, give it no read access to secret stores, and restrict it to the vendor's network where the cloud allows.

== Break the chain; keep build secrets from pushable branches
layout: compare
col: No single credential opens the next door | Each trust boundary's secrets in its own store | No external identity reads a secret store at all | The page: alert when an identity maps its permissions, then reads many secrets
col: Build secrets away from pushable branches | Deployment secrets in environments that admit only protected branches | OpenID Connect, so the pipeline holds no long-lived cloud key | Scope source-code tokens; alert on new workflow files on minutes-old branches
The workflow alert is the page's suggestion.
notes:
Two defenses for the chain itself.

Make sure no single credential opens the next door. Here an outside party's key could read secrets that included AWS and Azure credentials, and the chain ran on through a data analytics system, a source-code token and Kubernetes. The page says to keep each trust boundary's secrets in its own store, readable only by the workloads that use them, and to give no external identity read access to a secret store at all. It suggests alerting when an identity maps its own permissions and then reads many secrets in a short window.

Keep build secrets away from branches anyone with a token can push. In GitHub Actions, keep deployment secrets in environments whose deployment branch rules admit only protected branches, and use OpenID Connect so the pipeline holds no long-lived cloud key to print. Scope source-code tokens so they cannot push to repositories their owner does not need. Alert on new workflow files on branches that live for minutes and, as the CSA note suggests, on encoded or high-entropy strings in job logs.

The page calls Barracuda's published file and branch names a place to start, not a signature, since ARTEX circulates in forks and variants.

== Refuse host mounts; deny new admin identities
layout: compare
col: Refuse host mounts at admission | Enforce the baseline Pod Security Standard: no hostPath, no privileged pods | Alert on any pod spec that asks for a host path | Amazon EKS: require IMDSv2 and a hop limit of 1, per AWS
col: A preventive deny on new admin identities | Deny creating admin identities and attaching privileged policies | Except through a break-glass path with a second approver (CSA) | The page: alert on every denied attempt
notes:
Two defenses for the cluster and the cloud identity.

Refuse host mounts at admission, and keep node credentials away from pods. A read-only container that mounted a server's file system was enough to harvest that server's credentials. The baseline Pod Security Standard forbids hostPath volumes and privileged pods; enforce it, or an equivalent admission policy, on every namespace that does not need host access, and alert on any pod spec that asks for a host path. Barracuda is restricting privileged workloads, limiting which services can read secrets, and has deployed runtime threat detection. On Amazon EKS, AWS's best-practices guide shows how to keep pods that do not need the node's instance profile from reaching it, by requiring IMDSv2 and setting the hop limit to 1, and the CSA note recommends metadata session tokens and hop limits for the same reason.

Put a preventive deny in front of new administrative identities. The attacker's attempt was blocked, and Barracuda does not say by what. Make that block deliberate: an organization-wide policy that denies creating administrative identities and attaching privileged policies except through a break-glass path with a second approver, as the CSA note recommends. The alert on every denied attempt is the page's suggestion, because a denial shows a stolen credential trying to escalate.

== Log reads of credential stores; tell others what was in reach
layout: compare
col: Log reads where credentials are stored | Kubernetes: an audit policy that records reads of Secret objects | Key Vault: data-plane reads logged only with diagnostic logging on | Barracuda: full audit logging; data-access logging where missing
col: Tell others what was in reach | The vendor called, though it thought Barracuda unaffected | Hold others' credentials? Tell them which were in reach | Barracuda: share indicators and context early, even when unsure
The Kubernetes and Key Vault logging advice is the page's suggestion.
notes:
Two defenses for what happens after.

Log reads where credentials are stored, before you have to answer for them. Barracuda found no direct evidence that customers' XDR integration credentials were read, but logging in that environment was not detailed enough to rule it out, so customers had to rotate as a precaution. Barracuda has since enabled full audit logging on the affected environments, is adding data-access logging where it was missing, and is moving the credentials to a store that stays protected even if cluster administration is compromised. The page suggests that where credentials live in Kubernetes Secrets, an audit policy recording reads of Secret objects answers the question, and that Azure Key Vault records data-plane reads only once diagnostic logging is turned on.

Tell a customer what was in reach, even if you think they were not hit. The vendor did not believe Barracuda had been affected and contacted it anyway, the same day an engineer reported unusual activity, and Barracuda calls that the most useful outside signal it received. If you hold another company's credentials and your systems are breached, tell it which of its credentials were in reach. Barracuda urges sharing indicators and context early, even when unsure who is affected.

== Hunting: the indicators Barracuda published
layout: table
row: Indicator | As Barracuda publishes it
row: Resource names | "i" followed by digits, no hyphen: i31911, i28324probe, i19engx1
row: GitHub workflow files | i19ghci.yml, i19ci2.yml; branches i19ghci- and i19ci2-
row: IOC addresses | 180.178.56[.]10, 52.128.238[.]126, 221.146.68[.]107, 2a09:bac1:7680[:]3d0::26b:5b
row: User agents | Boto3/1.43.122, curl/8.5.0, curl/7.81.0, Python-urllib/3.10, "Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; read-only-audit)"
Barracuda: these indicators do not by themselves confirm malicious activity. The page: the file and branch names are a place to start, not a signature.
notes:
These are the markers the page gives, as Barracuda's advisory publishes them, with its caveat: the presence of these indicators does not by itself confirm malicious activity.

The naming fingerprints are cloud and Kubernetes resources whose names begin with the letter i followed by digits and no hyphen, and, in GitHub, the two workflow files on branches with the two prefixes. Barracuda calls these consistent with publicly reported characteristics of ARTEX.

The addresses are written defanged, as the page writes them. IOC stands for indicator of compromise. The user agents include Boto3, the AWS SDK for Python; the page reads that as a sign some of the activity called AWS APIs, and labels it its own inference, because the indicators are not tied to steps.

The page's own caveat on the file and branch names is that they are a place to start, not a signature, since ARTEX circulates in forks and variants. The FBI's ARTEX FLASH adds more indicators, along with mitigations that include continuous scanning of repositories, build artifacts and pipelines for exposed secrets, and prompt rotation of anything exposed.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Which cloud keys do vendors hold, and does any outlive its engagement?
check: Can you show a vendor key's last use and revoke it when work ends?
check: Can any external identity read a secret store?
check: Can a newly pushed branch's workflow read your build secrets?
check: Would your clusters admit a pod that mounts the host's file system?
check: Is creating an administrative identity denied outside a break-glass path?
check: Could you prove from logs that a stored credential was not read?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask which cloud keys, API tokens and service principal secrets your vendors, contractors and AI service providers hold, and whether any is still valid after the work it was issued for. Ask whether you could show a key's last use from the record, and whether anyone revokes it the day the engagement ends.

Ask whether any identity from outside your organization can read a secret store, and whether a workflow on a newly pushed branch can read the secrets your deployments use.

Ask whether your clusters would admit a pod that mounts the host's file system, and whether creating an administrative identity is denied except through a break-glass path with a second approver.

Finally, ask whether your logs could show that a stored credential was not read. Barracuda could not show it for the store holding customers' XDR credentials, so its customers rotated as a precaution.

== Would a key you gave a vendor still work today?
layout: question
Barracuda gave an AI vendor a cloud key for a project and did not retire it; the vendor's compromise exposed it. Pick one you issued to a vendor. When does it stop working, and who would notice its first use?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

The page's facts: as part of a previous project, Barracuda gave a cloud access key to a vendor it describes only as a company working in AI, and did not retire the key when the work ended. In October the vendor's development environment was compromised, and the key, stored in a configuration file in the vendor's GitHub repository, was exposed. On November 1 an attacker made its first call with it. Barracuda does not say what kind of key it was, what it could reach or why it was not retired.

Ask each person to pick one credential they have issued to a vendor, contractor or AI service provider, and say two things before you show the answer slide: the date it stops working, and the alert, if any, that its first use from somewhere new would raise.

If someone's answer is that it stops working when someone remembers to revoke it, that is the gap this chain started from.

== Expire it with the work, or make it short-lived
layout: answer
From the page's defenses:
- Tie the key's expiry to the engagement's end, and check its last use.
- Prefer short-lived access through federation to a standing key.
- Scope any static key: no secret-store reads, vendor's network where the cloud allows.
notes:
The page's answer has three parts, and the notes keep track of whose each one is.

First, expiry. Barracuda's remediation now includes expiring credentials held by vendors when their engagements end. The page suggests setting that expiry when the credential is issued, tied to the engagement's end date, and checking last-used data before then. The CSA note adds: inventory every long-lived key held outside your control, and revoke, not merely disable, credentials tied to completed engagements.

Second, the shape of the access. Barracuda says short-lived, narrowly scoped credentials would have stopped this intrusion at its first step. On the page's reading, a credential issued through federation each time the vendor needs it leaves nothing long-lived to find.

Third, scope. Where a static key cannot be avoided, scope it to the resources the project needs, give it no read access to secret stores, and restrict it to the vendor's network where the cloud allows.

Bring the group's answers back to these.

== What would you add for stage 07?
layout: question
None of the page's eight defenses counters stage 07, where shell commands searched servers for wallet material and found none. What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the empty column in the second defense matrix. The first matrix has another gap, stage 02, the tooling itself.

At stage 07, on a group of servers where it gained shell access, the attacker's commands searched for environment variables named WALLET, MNEMONIC, SEED and PRIV, Ethereum keystore files, extended private keys, seed phrase files, and Kubernetes secrets with wallet-related names. There was no wallet material to find. Barracuda does not say how the attacker gained shell access or on which servers.

Barracuda reads the activity as financially motivated, and says a third motive cannot be ruled out: some of the secrets in reach were credentials for AI services.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught: the shell access, the environment variable names, the file searches or the Kubernetes secrets.

== The page's defenses do not counter stage 07
layout: answer
This deck adds none. The stage's detect line is the page's only stage 07 suggestion:
- Alert on commands that list environment variables on servers that never handle wallets.
- Alert on searches for keystore and seed phrase file names there.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. None of the page's eight defenses counters stage 07, and this deck adds none of its own.

The stage's detect line is the page's suggestion: commands that list environment variables, or search for keystore and seed phrase file names, on servers that never handle wallets.

The group may point to the logging defense, which suggests an audit policy that records reads of Kubernetes Secret objects. The page ties that defense to the store holding customers' XDR credentials at stage 10, not to the wallet search, so keep that argument the group's own.

The group may also argue that the chain should have been broken earlier, at stage 01 or 04. The page ties those defenses to those stages, not to stage 07.

== Primary sources
layout: links
link: Barracuda, Forty-four hours: anatomy of a machine-speed intrusion | https://blog.barracuda.com/2026/11/11/anatomy-of-a-machine-speed-intrusion
link: Barracuda Trust Center, XDR integration credentials | https://trust.barracuda.com/security/barracuda-xdr-integration-credentials
link: BleepingComputer, Barracuda links cloud breach to ARTEX | https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/barracuda-links-cloud-breach-to-artex-ai-powered-cyberattack/
link: Cloud Security Alliance, research note on the intrusion | https://labs.cloudsecurityalliance.org/research/csa-research-note-barracuda-artex-machine-speed-intrusion-20/
link: Railway, November 10 incident report | https://blog.railway.com/p/incident-report-october-security-incident
link: FBI, FLASH on the ARTEX framework | https://www.ic3.gov/CSA/2026/261113.pdf
link: Kubernetes, Pod Security Standards | https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/
link: GitHub Docs, secure use reference (log redaction) | https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use
notes:
These are primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

Barracuda's CISO wrote the November 11 post, and the Trust Center advisory was published on November 10 and updated on November 13. BleepingComputer reported on the ARTEX link. The Cloud Security Alliance's research note rests on Barracuda's posts; the page notes that its heading names a different company, Fireworks AI, though its text is about Barracuda. Railway's report is the one Barracuda compares its intrusion with. The FBI's FLASH describes ARTEX and does not mention Barracuda.

The page also cites AWS's Amazon EKS best practices, GitHub's documentation on deployment environments and OpenID Connect, the GitHub Actions runner source, and MITRE ATT&CK pages for four techniques. They are left off this slide for space.

The background slides name their own sources: MITRE ATT&CK and the Kubernetes documentation.

== Related kill chains and defense topics
layout: links
link: ARTEX exposed-secrets kill chain | breaches/artex-github-secrets-azure.html
link: VAPOR PANDA / ARTEX | breaches/vapor-panda-artex.html
link: Storm-0940 and ConnectWise | breaches/storm-0940-connectwise.html
link: Black October / AI developer platforms | breaches/black-october-ai-platforms.html
link: Non-human identity | non-human-identity.html
link: CI/CD security | ci-cd.html
link: Kubernetes security | kubernetes.html
link: Cloud logging | cloud-logging.html
No source connects this intrusion to the other ARTEX chains, and Barracuda and Railway do not say the same attacker was involved.
notes:
The page links four other kill chains. No source ties this intrusion to the other ARTEX chains, or to the same attacker as any of them; Barracuda compares only the shape and motive of its chain with Railway's, one of the Black October platforms.

In the exposed-secrets campaign Microsoft describes, an agentic framework it links to ARTEX searched GitHub for secrets. Barracuda's key also sat in a GitHub repository, but Barracuda says it was exposed through the vendor's compromised development environment, and neither Microsoft nor Barracuda links the two. VAPOR PANDA is CrowdStrike's name for a China-nexus actor that used ARTEX in October, and Storm-0940 is Microsoft's name for another; neither appears in Barracuda's account.

Black October covers the AI developer platforms that disclosed incidents in October, Railway among them. Barracuda compares the shape of its chain and the motive with Railway's. Railway's report describes defending against "a harness similar to ARTEX, according to Wiz" (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz).

The defense topics cover non-human identity, CI/CD security, Kubernetes security and cloud logging. The page also links IAM and incident response, left off for space.
